@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Show the numbers") {
        The task app tracks state; now make it show the numbers. Add a
        Stats tab that presents the same store four ways: a ``Table`` for
        exact values, a ``Sparkline`` for shape over time, a ``BarChart``
        for comparison, and a ``Gauge`` for a single ratio.

        Continue from Give It Structure's finished code, or
        [download the finished project](https://dardevelin.github.io/terminal-ui/downloads/build-a-task-list.zip)
        as the base to edit.

        @Image(source: "demo-ch5.gif", alt: "Opening the stats tab: table, sparkline, bars, and gauge.")
    }

    @Section(title: "A table of the facts") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ``Table`` renders rows as aligned columns: each ``TableColumn``
            pairs a header, a width, and a closure mapping a row to its cell
            string. It is display-only — no cursor, no focus — and the whole
            table precomputes into one text node, which is why it stays
            cheap at hundreds of rows. The interactive list keeps its job;
            the table's job is exact values at a glance.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a third ``Tab`` — Stats — holding a `StatsPane` with a
                ``Table`` of the tasks. Two columns: the title, verbatim,
                and a status derived per row. The closures pull whatever
                string they like out of a `TaskItem`; the table never needs
                your model to know it is being displayed.

                Toggle a task with Enter, press `3`: the Status column
                already knows, because the table's rows are `store.tasks`
                read fresh each render.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks5-01-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks5-01-01.png", alt: "The Stats tab: a two-column table of tasks with a Status column reading done or open.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "The shape of history") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A table answers "what is the value"; charts answer everything
            else. ``Sparkline`` plots a series onto a ``BrailleCanvas`` —
            every cell is a 2×4 grid of braille dots, so a 40×3-cell strip
            resolves 80×12 points — and ``BarChart`` compares labeled
            values with eighth-block precision. Both are views like any
            other: feed them from the store and they follow it.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Seed a `history` array on the store — completions per day —
                and render it as a ``Sparkline``, then compare open against
                done in a ``BarChart``. Pin the sparkline's `min:` so the
                axis stays put as data arrives, and give both charts a fixed
                frame — they are flexible by default, and an unbounded chart
                would take the leftover rows.

                Toggle a task on the Tasks tab and come back: the bars move.
                No chart-update code exists — the bars are a function of the
                store, like every other view in the app.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks5-02-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks5-02-01.png", alt: "The Stats tab: the table, a braille sparkline of completions, and an open-versus-done bar chart.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "One number that matters") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            When one ratio is the headline, draw it as a ``Gauge`` — a value
            sitting within its range, free to move both ways, unlike a
            ``ProgressBar``'s promise of an end state. That completes the
            vocabulary: ``Table`` for exact values, ``Sparkline`` for shape
            over time, ``BarChart`` for comparison, ``Gauge`` for a single
            ratio. Pick by the question the screen answers, not by which
            view looks fanciest.

            Up next: <doc:MakeItProduction> — confirmation dialogs,
            async loading, and the debug tools.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a `completion` ratio to the store and a ``Gauge``
                beneath the charts. The gauge takes a plain value, not a
                binding — it reports state the app owns, so there is
                nothing on it to operate.

                One store, four questions answered: which tasks (table),
                how the pace looks (sparkline), open versus done (bars),
                how far along (gauge).

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks5-03-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks5-03-01.png", alt: "The finished Stats tab: table, sparkline, bar chart, and a Done gauge reading 0.33.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            The app should show how request latency has moved over the last
            minute. Which view fits?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                A ``Sparkline`` fed the rolling samples.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Shape over time is the sparkline's question — the eye
                    reads trend and spikes from the line, not from digits.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                A ``Table`` with one row per sample.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A table gives exact values, but sixty rows of numbers
                    hide the trend a one-line sparkline makes obvious.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                A ``Gauge`` of the latest sample.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A gauge shows where one value sits in its range right
                    now — the minute of history disappears.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How does a ``Table`` turn a `TaskItem` into the text of a cell?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Each ``TableColumn`` carries a closure mapping a row to its
                cell string.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    The column owns the projection — `{ $0.done ? "done" :
                    "open" }` — so the model never has to know it is being
                    displayed.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                The row type conforms to a protocol that names its columns.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The only requirement on rows is `Identifiable`; columns
                    are defined at the call site, per table.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                The table reflects over the row's properties and prints
                each one.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Nothing is inferred — you choose the columns, their
                    widths, and exactly how each cell string is derived.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why do the charts update when a task is toggled on another tab,
            with no chart-update code anywhere?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Their bodies read the `@Observable` store, so the mutation
                re-renders them like any other view.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    A chart is just a view: state drives it the same way it
                    drives the list — one store, every reader follows.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Charts poll the store on a timer.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Nothing polls — an idle app does zero work. The render
                    is triggered by the state mutation itself.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                The tab switch rebuilds the whole app, so the charts start
                fresh.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The charts would update even while visible, no tab
                    switch needed — observation, not reconstruction, keeps
                    them current.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
